  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nFriday - Saturday...\n\nThe weak surface boundary will be stalled somewhere near the KY/TN \nborder on Friday, with a weak and meandering shortwave over the mid \nMississippi River Valley. A high PWAT airmass will pool over our \narea ahead of this weak upper trough axis with showers and storm \nchances continuing mainly each afternoon and evening. The overall \ndeep layer shear profile will be weak, so any slow moving storm may \nbe capable of localized heavy rain given the high moisture content. \n1000-1500 J/KG of CAPE in a tall/skinny profile combined with 2 - \n2.25\" PWATS, high freezing levels above 15K feet, and slow movement \nwill be quite efficient with rain rates. Something to watch going \ninto the first part of the weekend.\n\nTemperatures each day will be mostly in the low and mid 80s, \nalthough the southern and western CWA could sneak into the upper \n80s. Will keep pops pretty high through this period given the amount \nof deeper moisture and a triggering mechanism. \n\nSunday - Tuesday...\n\nLooks to be a drier and hotter stretch of weather for the end of the \nweekend going into early next week. The southern CONUS ridge will \nbuild back over the area, pushing the stronger westerlies much \nfarther north. This will put us in a mostly subsident and dry \npattern as forecast soundings do show an impressive H7 inversion. \nHotter temperatures will also accompany this upper pattern change \nwith afternoon highs back in the upper 80s and low 90s by Tuesday.\n\nWednesday...\n\nThere seems to be fairly decent model agreement (for Day 7 anyway), \nthat the upper ridge breaks down a bit by mid week, and allows a \ncold front to approach our region. This would bring a return of \nshowers and thunderstorms to the area, if correct. In addition, \ntemperatures would probably step back toward normal temps back in \nthe 80s.",
